  "worked_solution": "We have that the currency $E(D)$ is measured in euros and the currency $D$ is measured in dollars. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(D): & \\text{euros} \\\\ D: & \\text{dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ measures a change in $E(D)$ over a change in $D.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}: & \\frac{\\text{euros}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ is measured in \"euros per dollar\".",
